WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.568
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.568 --> 00:00:09.640
Think about a defining
moment in your life.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.670
A moment that had a major
impact on who you are today.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.680
A moment that made you
rethink your priorities.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.150
I wonder how many of you are thinking
about a moment of adversity.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.410
Maybe you involved in an accident.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.250
We left a broken relationship.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.000
We have failed at something that
was really important to you.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.640
One such moment that made me
rethink what matters most to me,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.020
was also caused by adversity.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.664
It was December, 2000, 4 days before
Christmas, I was 13 years old and

00:00:43.664 --> 00:00:47.795
had bruises all over my body,
there were at least 50 of them.

00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:50.874
And when it started as a normal
nosebleed here and there,

00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:53.580
turned into something abnormal and
concerning.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.110
So went to my local doctor
who took a blood sample and

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.560
that same night I was
admitted to the hospital.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.773
A number of tests followed until
the doctors could tell my parents and

00:01:07.773 --> 00:01:10.028
me the diagnosis, leukemia.

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:14.460
And I could still very vividly remember
my first reaction hearing this.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.775
At least it's not cancer.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:19.036
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:22.275
my parents had to tell me that leukemia,
in fact, was cancer.

00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:24.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I was devastated.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.430
What followed were long
lines of chemotherapy.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.336
Every morning I got an infusion
of something that looked and

00:01:32.336 --> 00:01:34.532
smelled and indeed, was toxic.

00:01:34.532 --> 00:01:37.179
I lost all my energy, most of my hair and

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:42.090
some mornings I couldn't quite recognize
that face I saw in the mirror.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.180
And it turned out that chemotherapy
alone wouldn't do the job.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.480
I needed a stem cell transplant
from a genetic twin.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.290
No one in my family was a match, so the
doctors turned to the worldwide registries

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:58.230
of stem cell donors, and luckily,
they found a genetic twin.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.380
A stem cell transplant saved my life.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.650
But this is not the end of the story,
it's the beginning.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.900
This is not a talk about cancer,
it's a talk about life.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.240
Having survived cancer, my attitude
towards life completely changed.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.080
I realized my life is finite and
it's my own choice and

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.710
responsibility to make it matter.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.360
Going back to school was a blast.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.310
I enjoyed studying.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.360
Which is probably not the most
common thing for a 15 year old.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.190
Started writing for

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.340
a local news paper, joined a drama
group and found my passion for acting.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.580
I created an exhibit about
cancer to educate the public,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.130
together with a fellow patient.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.820
Traveled all across Europe and

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.620
couldn't care less what other
people thought about me.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.980
Overcoming cancer was a catalyst that
kept me pushing forward in life.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.310
It taught me lessons.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.700
Some people only learn when
their life is coming to an end.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.096
But now?

00:02:57.096 --> 00:02:59.963
More than a decade after surviving cancer,

00:02:59.963 --> 00:03:04.000
the clarity of this defining
experience started to wear off.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:09.910
While my attitude towards life is still
very much shape of that perspective that I

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:15.620
gained, lessons that I learned at
least present in everyday life and

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.410
an autopilot starting
to taking over again.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:24.394
So the past few years of spend far too
much time with people only talk energy but

00:03:24.394 --> 00:03:29.842
never gave me energy, I worry too much
about what other people think about me and

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:34.414
[SOUND] worked many hours and
treated my body with respect.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.160
Think about your own moments of adversity.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.270
How much of the choices you make
still shape that experience?

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.720
Since coming to Stanford, I had a lot
of time to reflect on my own life, and

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:47.610
I realized something.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.310
Lasting change in live doesn't come
through the occasional setback or crisis.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.573
Lasting change in life happens in
the small moments of each day.

00:03:58.573 --> 00:04:02.776
The small moments that give us
the opportunity to reflect on our lives,

00:04:02.776 --> 00:04:05.520
and reconfirm or
change who we want to become.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:11.377
So the other day, a close friend was
asking me, what's one thing in your life

00:04:11.377 --> 00:04:14.313
that you want to change and
what keeps you from doing it?

00:04:14.313 --> 00:04:17.053
And I said he knew
the answer being single.

00:04:17.053 --> 00:04:24.089
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:28.429
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Deep relations matter a lot to me.

00:04:28.429 --> 00:04:31.880
And in that moment, I felt inspired
to finally do something about it.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.200
But seconds later, the voices in my head
started shouting, this is a bad plan.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.050
Online dating is time consuming and

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.185
random and
there's a lot of creepy people on there.

00:04:42.185 --> 00:04:46.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Meeting new people,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.422
it can lead to rejection.

00:04:47.422 --> 00:04:53.357
And isn't complicated relationship
just more work than being alone?

00:04:53.357 --> 00:04:56.575
And this is exactly the pattern
that keeps repeating over and

00:04:56.575 --> 00:05:01.140
over again in the moments that can
be potentially be defining for us.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.460
You realize that you want to change
something, but then you stop.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.333
You realize that you want to finally
end that unhealthy relationship,

00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:13.690
quit that job you hate, spend more time
with that friend, start volunteering,

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:18.176
eat healthier, start working out, say I
love you to someone, whatever it is, but

00:05:18.176 --> 00:05:19.552
you don't take action.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:22.933
You realize and find this talk or
conversation or

00:05:22.933 --> 00:05:26.400
article aspiring, but
then that moment passes.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:31.120
Too often, we let that moment
slip through our fingers

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.490
because we don't feel
empowered to make a choice.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.030
We don't feel empowered to
overcome the obstacles.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.020
But in these moments,
we have to go further.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.060
Which would reflect on what's holding
us back and make a choice to act.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.300
So that conversation with
my friend got me thinking.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.610
Yes, I can find a thousand and
I'm creative so

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.520
probably even more reasons why dating
is time consuming and complicated.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.260
Hey, it's important to me so
it's worth the effort.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.850
The only way I can lose is better
than not doing anything at all.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:08.200
And looking at my own life I can find many
of these small moments where reflecting on

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.039
what's holding me back, and
making a choice to act,

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:14.210
can actually bring me a little
bit closer to who I want to be.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:19.510
So last year, for example, I spent a lot
of time recruiting for an internship and

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.800
consulting.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.950
Although deep down I knew that that's not
quite the thing I want to do with my life.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:29.000
But it seemed prestigious and other
people excited so I gave it a shot and

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.840
went to all these recruiting events and
sessions and

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.330
interviews and, well,
actually ended up with an offer letter.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.600
And holding that thing in my hand I
thought, well, it looks like a solid plan,

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.440
it would be stupid to turn that down.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:47.110
And I was this close to signing until I
stumbled upon my Stanford application

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.580
what matters most to me and why?

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.428
And we're reading that,

00:06:50.428 --> 00:06:55.650
I realized what should have known
before that spending time on consulting

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.250
wouldn't bring you any closer to that
Korean media that I want to have.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.230
So I took action and turn it down.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.630
At the end of last year,
I felt pretty exhausted.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:09.590
I over committed to too many classes and
I took projects and activities and

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:14.020
social commitments and all that stuff
we over commit to, many of you do.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.800
And I felt that my mind lost
it's usual clarity and focus.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.300
And seating on the airport, waiting for

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:23.710
my flight back home for winter break, felt
like I have to do something about this.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:29.230
But the only idea I had was
mediation because I remembered

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:33.850
conversations with friends, we talked
about the benefits of meditation.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.520
But I didn't quite like the idea
of sitting cross-legged and

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.828
focusing my breath like ten minutes and
hoping it will be better.

00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:44.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But truth be told,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.570
I didn't have any better idea.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:51.728
So I downloaded that meditation app that
friend recommended and tried it out.

00:07:51.728 --> 00:07:52.870
What can I say?

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.460
I'm doing it every day now,
and my mind is a lot clearer.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.010
I'm actually taking a meditation
class next quarter.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.850
And this list of small defining moments,
reflecting and

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.410
acting, can make a difference,
it's practically endless.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.247
And I'm not a master in acting
on all of these moments yet.

00:08:09.247 --> 00:08:11.847
I'm still figuring things out.

00:08:11.847 --> 00:08:13.920
But now I want you to
think about your own life.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.070
Think about yesterday,
what did you do, who did you talk to,

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:22.700
when did you feel inspired.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:28.382
What were the moments when you thought,
I should do this, or

00:08:28.382 --> 00:08:30.135
I should stop doing that.

00:08:30.135 --> 00:08:32.480
But you didn't take action.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.770
You lacked that conversation.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:36.760
You put that book aside.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:38.800
You closed your eyes.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:43.070
We think too much, and hold back too much.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:48.417
We want to do so many things in our lives,
but we don't even start,

00:08:48.417 --> 00:08:50.306
because we think we can wait.

00:08:50.306 --> 00:08:52.860
Don't wait for cancer.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.240
Don't wait for
adversity to hit you in the face.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.540
But the next time you think,
I should do this.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:02.590
Don't go back to your busy life.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.980
Don't go back to autopilot.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.694
But instead,
ask yourself what's holding you back.

00:09:09.694 --> 00:09:14.128
Reflect and act right now.

00:09:14.128 --> 00:09:18.656
Your defining moment can be right now.

00:09:18.656 --> 00:09:19.492
Thank you.

00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:23.742
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:23.742 --> 00:09:28.239
[MUSIC]

